PAGES=[page('086_page_1.jpg',163,[heading('6.5 Integration using partial fractions'),paragraph('Partial fraction decomposition has great value as a tool for integration. This process may be thought of as the “reverse” of adding fractional algebraic expressions, and it allows us to break up rational expressions into simpler terms. Partial fraction decomposition is an algebraic procedure for expressing a reduced rational function as a sum of fractional parts. For example, the rational expression'),eq(r'f(x)=\frac{P(x)}{D(x)}','i'),paragraph('can be decomposed into partial fractions only if P(x) and D(x) have no common factors and if the degree of P(x) is less than the degree of D(x). If the degree of P(x) is greater than or equal to the degree of D(x), then use division to obtain a polynomial plus a proper fraction. For example, the rational function after division is:'),eq(r'\frac{x^4+2x^3-4x^2+x-3}{x^2-x-2}=x^2+3x+1+\frac{8x-1}{x^2-x-2}','ii'),paragraph('x²+3x+1 is our polynomial term. (8x−1)/(x²−x−2) is our proper fraction (this is the part which requires decomposition into partial fractions).'),paragraph('In algebra, the theory of equations tells us that any polynomial P(x) with real coefficients can be expressed as a product of linear and irreducible quadratic powers, some of which may be repeated. This fact can be used to justify the following general procedure for obtaining the partial fraction decomposition of a rational function.'),paragraph('Let f(x) = P(x)/D(x), where P(x) and D(x) have no common factors and D(x) ≠ 0.'),heading('The steps involved in decomposing the rational function are the following:'),paragraph('1. If the degree of P(x) is greater than or equal to the degree of D(x), use long division to express P(x)/D(x) as the sum of a polynomial and a fraction R(x)/D(x) in which the degree of the remainder polynomial R(x) is less than the degree of the denominator polynomial D(x).'),paragraph('2. Factorize the denominator D(x) into the product of linear and irreducible quadratic powers.'),paragraph('3. Express P(x)/D(x) as a cascading sum of partial fractions of the form'),eq(r'\frac{A_i}{(x-r)^i}\quad\mathrm{and}\quad\frac{A_j+B_k}{(x^2+sx+t)^j}'),paragraph('Verify that the number of constants used is identical to the degree of the denominator.'),note('Cancel common factors first while retaining exclusions from the original denominator; division/cancellation does not prevent decomposition. The irreducible quadratic numerator must be linear, Ax+B: the printed general-form numerator Aⱼ+Bₖ omits x. Include every power from 1 up to the multiplicity of each factor.'),heading('6.5.1 Use of partial fraction to find ∫f(x)/g(x) dx where f(x) and g(x) are algebraic functions, such that g(x) ≠ 0'),heading('Example 13'),paragraph(r'Evaluate (a) \(\int\frac{8x-1}{x^2-x-2}\,dx\); (b) \(\int\frac{x^2-6x+3}{(x-2)^3}\,dx\); (c) \(\int\frac{2x^3+x^2+2x+4}{(x^2+1)^2}\,dx\).'),heading('Solution'),eq(r'\mathrm{a.}\quad\int\frac{8x-1}{x^2-x-2}\,dx'),paragraph('The integrand is a proper fraction, so we start by factoring the denominator x²−x−2 = (x−2)(x+1). The denominator factors are the two distinct linear factors, so we can set the rational function equal to the sum of the two partial fractions'),eq(r'\frac{8x-1}{x^2-x-2}=\frac{A_1}{x-2}+\frac{A_2}{x+1}','i')
]),page('086_page_2.jpg',164,[paragraph('To determine the constants A₁ and A₂, we multiply both sides of the equation (i) by (x−2)(x+1) to obtain:'),eq(r'8x-1=A_1(x+1)+A_2(x-2)','ii'),paragraph('Set x−2 = 0 ⇒ x = 2 in equation (ii) to obtain:'),eq(r'8(2)-1=A_1(2+1)+A_2(2-2)\Rightarrow15=3A_1\Rightarrow A_1=5'),paragraph('Set x+1 = 0 ⇒ x = −1 in equation (ii) to obtain:'),eq(r'8(-1)-1=A_1(-1+1)+A_2(-1-2)\Rightarrow-9=-3A_2\Rightarrow A_2=3'),paragraph('Use the constants values in equation (i) to obtain:'),eq(r'\frac{8x-1}{x^2-x-2}=\frac{A_1}{x-2}+\frac{A_2}{x+1}=\frac5{x-2}+\frac3{x+1}'),paragraph('Use this decomposition instead of rational expression in the given integral to obtain:'),eq(r'\int\frac{8x-1}{x^2-x-2}\,dx=\int\left[\frac5{x-2}+\frac3{x+1}\right]\,dx=\int\frac5{x-2}\,dx+\int\frac3{x+1}\,dx'),eq(r'=5\ln(x-2)+3\ln(x+1)+\ln C=\ln(x-2)^5+\ln(x+1)^3+\ln C=\ln C(x-2)^5(x+1)^3'),heading('b.'),eq(r'\int\frac{x^2-6x+3}{(x-2)^3}\,dx'),paragraph('The integrand is a proper fraction, so we start by factoring the denominator (x−2)³ = (x−2)(x−2)(x−2). The denominator factors are the three repeated linear factors, so we can set the rational function equal to the sum of the three partial fractions'),eq(r'\frac{x^2-6x+3}{(x-2)^3}=\frac{A_1}{x-2}+\frac{A_2}{(x-2)^2}+\frac{A_3}{(x-2)^3}','i'),paragraph('To determine the constants A₁,A₂ and A₃, we multiply both sides of the equation (i) by (x−2)³ to obtain:'),eq(r'x^2-6x+3=A_1(x-2)^2+A_2(x-2)+A_3=A_1(x^2-4x+4)+A_2(x-2)+A_3','ii'),paragraph('Set x−2 = 0 ⇒ x = 2 in equation (ii) to obtain:'),eq(r'2^2-6(2)+3=A_1(2-2)^2+A_2(2-2)+A_3\Rightarrow-5=A_3\Rightarrow A_3=-5'),paragraph('For constants A₁,A₂, we equate the coefficients of x² and x on each side of equation (ii) to obtain:'),eq(r'1=A_1\quad(x^2\mathrm{\ terms}),\qquad-6=-4A_1+A_2\quad(x\mathrm{\ terms})'),paragraph('Solving this system of equations for the unknowns A₁ and A₂ to obtain A₁ = 1 and A₂ = −2. Use these constants values in equation (i) to obtain:'),eq(r'\frac{x^2-6x+3}{(x-2)^3}=\frac1{x-2}-\frac2{(x-2)^2}-\frac5{(x-2)^3}'),paragraph('Use this decomposition instead of rational expression in the given integral to obtain:'),eq(r'\int\frac{x^2-6x+3}{(x-2)^3}\,dx=\int\left[\frac1{x-2}-\frac2{(x-2)^2}-\frac5{(x-2)^3}\right]\,dx'),eq(r'=\ln(x-2)-2\frac{(x-2)^{-2+1}}{-2+1}-5\frac{(x-2)^{-3+1}}{-3+1}+C=\ln(x-2)+\frac2{x-2}+\frac5{2(x-2)^2}+C'),note('For (a), use 5ln|x−2|+3ln|x+1|+C on each interval excluding −1 and 2. In (b), use ln|x−2|+2/(x−2)+5/[2(x−2)²]+C for x ≠ 2. The printed logarithms without absolute values cover only a restricted branch; writing ln C also restricts that constant to positive C.'),heading('c.'),eq(r'\int\frac{2x^3+x^2+2x+4}{(x^2-1)^2}\,dx'),paragraph('The integrand is a proper fraction and the denominator factors are the two repeated quadratic factors, so we can set the rational function equal to the sum of the two partial fractions:'),eq(r'\frac{2x^3+x^2+2x+4}{(x^2+1)^2}=\frac{A_1x+B_1}{(x^2+1)^2}+\frac{A_2x+B_2}{x^2+1}','i'),paragraph('To determine the constants values, the similar procedure is used to obtain A₁ = 0,A₂ = 2,B₁ = 3,B₂ = 1.'),note('The displayed integral at the start of (c) prints (x²−1)²; the original question and every decomposition use (x²+1)². The learning solution follows the original question.')
]),page('087_page_1.jpg',165,[paragraph('With these substitutions, the equation (i) becomes:'),eq(r'\frac{2x^3+x^2+2x+4}{(x^2+1)^2}=\frac3{(x^2+1)^2}+\frac{2x+1}{x^2+1}','ii'),paragraph('Integrate this decomposition to obtain:'),eq(r'\int\frac{2x^3+x^2+2x+4}{(x^2+1)^2}\,dx=\int\frac3{(x^2+1)^2}\,dx+\int\frac{2x+1}{x^2+1}\,dx'),eq(r'=\int\frac3{(x^2+1)^2}\,dx+\int\frac{2x}{x^2+1}\,dx+\int\frac1{x^2+1}\,dx'),paragraph('Now the readers are in position, how to find the complete solution of the question.'),heading('Hint'),eq(r'\int\frac1{x^2+1}\,dx=\tan^{-1}x,\quad\int\frac{2x}{x^2+1}\,dx=\ln u,\quad u=x^2+1'),eq(r'\int\frac3{(x^2+1)^2}\,dx=?,\quad x=\tan\theta,\quad dx=\sec^2\theta'),note('The source stops at a hint for part (c). Completing its remaining integral gives ln(x²+1)+(5/2)arctan x+3x/[2(x²+1)]+C. This is a separate SpaceBook learning completion, not a claimed printed source result.')])]
